---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import CardBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/CardBuild.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="card-style-queries">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Shadows barely show on dark surfaces, so an elevated card needs a heavier
      one in dark mode. <code>light-dark()</code> only picks colors, and a shadow
      is offsets, blur and color together.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> media query only knows the system setting.
      It misses a page forced to dark with a class, and a dark section inside a light
      page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The theme sets <code>--color-scheme: dark</code> wherever it switches to dark,
        and custom properties inherit
      </li>
      <li>
        Every element is a style container, so <code
          >@container style(--color-scheme: dark)</code
        > asks the card's parent which scheme it's in
      </li>
      <li>
        Variants only swap custom properties, the shadow included, so the query
        changes one value
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.dark {
  --color-scheme: dark;
  color-scheme: dark;
}

.card {
  --shadow-elevated: var(--shadow-3);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow, none);

  @container style(--color-scheme: dark) {
    --shadow-elevated: var(--shadow-4);
  }
}

.card.elevated {
  --card-shadow: var(--shadow-elevated);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <CardBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Browsers without style queries skip the <code>@container</code> block
      </li>
      <li>
        Elevated cards keep the lighter shadow in dark mode: flatter, but
        unchanged otherwise
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="actions">Actions</h2>
    <p>
      The actions row looks at its contents instead, with <code>:has()</code>. <code
        >.ui-button:first-child[class="ui-button"]</code
      > only matches a plain text button, with no variant class. It has no background,
      so the row moves towards the start to line its label up with the content.
    </p>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
